[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Independent sdoc graphs per family, stitched by UID after validation

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[WORK]
UID: WORK-INDEPENDENT-FAMILY-GRAPHS
TITLE: Independent sdoc graphs per family, stitched by UID after validation
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
The operator's idea for the grammar redesign, raised 2026-09-08 while shaping the cycle policy: annotations may point at anything, work points at itself and at spec, narratives cite anything, so any cross-family relation pulls the whole graph into one export and one acyclicity question. The proposal is to make each family its own sdoc graph, validated on its own (acyclic, resolvable), with cross-family references not being strictdoc relations at all, and to stitch the graphs back together by UID only in custom tools: the semantic validation and the render tools. Open readings of 'independent', not yet chosen: separate strictdoc roots per family with their own exports; one root where cross-family references are a plain field strictdoc never resolves; or the current single export with the cycle check run per family. What moves with the split: fingerprints from work to spec, the readiness closure, fp-check and the cycle policy all run on the stitched graph, and the stitch tool owns the dangling-reference check and reverse links strictdoc provides today. MIDs are assumed to be computed differently per graph; UIDs stay globally unique and are the stitch key.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Operator, 2026-09-08: "may be moot. Annotations can point to anything. Work can point to self and spec. I can't see a scenerio where the full graph doesnt get pulled in unless we drop spec/work relations to indirect nodes from annotations and like wise spec from work. Narrative has the same problem to. Like we drop all spec nodes not directly related to an annotation. Independent sdoc graphs. Then post acyclic validation on each stitch it back together based on UIDs or something. Assuming MIDs will be computed differently. Stitching may only happen for like custom semantic validation or render tools."
<<<
